    "q-212521": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212521",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that $-21≡-12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=9$\n- $n=12$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-21-(-12)=-9.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷3=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷9=-1,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $12∤(-9).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
